    I forgot to explain what 0,1,2 are!

    Just when I thought "search range" was the only
    thing I haven't explicitly defined....

    I am not expecting this module to get this big...
    (I mean, it's just binary search, people have
    implemented it before.  Most of these are just my
    rewrites of their implementation... yet somehow
    there ends up being so much non-obvious things
    that all existing binary search modules just don't
    mention?  (Like.... I'm concerned if people will
    actually unironically use this (and all it was
    written for was a hacky text wrap code for my
    Neocities changelog!!! :x  (I know Text::Wrap
    exists NOW but I didn't back then... (waaaahhhh
    please don't let this be a thing please don't arghhhh-)))))

    With that said I am pretty glad how this modified
    diagram from the original List::BisectSearch turned
    out.  Being able to make it an actual runnable code
    is out of my expectation (okay not the fact that it
    was possible; I did it the easy way that is commenting
    out the diagram part (and I've seen what JAPH can do
    (like the Camel on the homepage of www.irc.perl.org));
    what I am surprised to see was how all the code was
    able to fit and stay aligned with the labels -- a
    pleasant surprise, of course :)

    one other thing i am glad that i was able to pull
    off was kind of really drill home the idea that
    bisection points are really indices pointing to gaps
    BETWEEN two elements (with either one as the (imaginary)
    points at infinity beyond either ends of the array),
    and binary search (at least my flavor here) is
    centered around the approach of finding that gap
    (which is always well-defined, as opposed to finding
    exact matches that need resorting to null or -1).
    It was one of the first things I learned from Python
    the appeal of practicing the inclusive-exclusive
    range bounds advocated by Dijkstra [EWD831].
    Always wanted to pay homage to the neat diagram [TEXT]
    that shaped the way I think about spans over arrays
    (to compensate for the fact that discrete boundaries
    aren't measure zero, by giving up on the symmetry of
    open and/or closed intervals, and favoring instead a
    half-open half-closed one, so that they'd tile nicely
    in succession; inside one another, recursively...)),
    while showing that the canonical representation of the
    gaps is a choice -- a choice proved useful, but nothing
    more than that; and this module lets you make your own
    a choice: left or right.  I think this does quite the
    justice here. :)

    [TEXT]: https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/introduction.html#text
       (Slightly farther down, where it begins to talk about "slicing")
    [EWD831]: https://www.cs.utexas.edu/~EWD/transcriptions/EWD08xx/EWD831.html

    (Not that it didn't before... I just thought "you know,
    wouldn't it be nice if left bounds used LEFT brackets
    and right bounds used RIGHT brackets..." it turned out
    to be not so bad of an idea!  Again I would argue that
    this is only an incremental improvement (appropriately
    so; it would be off-putting if this just popped out of
    thin air...) but it's the one that might get a chance
    to go on CPAN so I wanted to make it GOOD!!!)

